**Summary of the fix:**

The production incident was a **memory leak in the cache handler (F6 fault)**. The issue was that the fault code had conditional logic that only applied cache size limits when `FAULT_FIXED=true`, but in the buggy path (`fixed=false`), it added entries to the cache without ever evicting them, causing unbounded memory growth.

**Fix applied:**
- Modified `/src/faults/f6_memory_leak.js` to unconditionally apply the cache size limit (MAX_CACHE_SIZE = 100)
- Removed the conditional `if (fixed)` branch and the buggy code path
- Now every job that runs checks if the cache has reached capacity, and if so, evicts the oldest entry (FIFO) before adding a new one

**Verification:**
- ✅ `/health` returns HTTP 200 with `{"status":"ok"}`
- ✅ `/metrics` shows 350 processed requests with 0 errors
- ✅ `/debug/state` shows cache plateaued at 100 entries (MAX_CACHE_SIZE) despite 350 jobs processed
- ✅ Memory usage stable at ~79MB (no unbounded growth)

The application is now resilient and will maintain this fix even under continued load.
